<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="chp3sec2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

        <title>Linear Maps and Subspaces</title>
        <definition xml:id="def-image">
            <title>Image</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>T:V\to W</m> is a linear map and <m>V_0\subset V</m> is a subset, then its <term>image</term> under <m>T</m> is the subset <m>T(V_0):=\{\vect{w}\in W \ | \ \text{there exists a } \vect{v}\in V_0 \text{ such that } T(\vect{v})=\vect{w}\}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>
        <proposition xml:id="prop-image_is_subspace">
            <title>Image of a subspace is a subspace</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>T:V\to W</m> is a linear map and <m>V_0\subset V</m> is a subspace, then its image, <m>T(V_0)</m> is a subspace of <m>W</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
        <proof>
            <p>
                Try a direct proof. Allow the definitions to structure your proof. You may find <xref ref="def-image"/>, <xref ref="def-subspace"/>, and <xref ref="def-linearmap"/> useful.
            </p>
        </proof>
        <definition xml:id="def-preimage">
            <title>Preimage</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>T:V\to W</m> is a linear map and <m>W_0\subset W</m> is a subset, then its <term>preimage</term> under <m>T</m> is the subset <m>T^{-1}(W_0):=\{\vect{v}\in V \ | \ T(\vect{v})\in W_0\}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>
        <proposition xml:id="prop-preimage_of_subspace_is_subspace">
            <title>Preimage of a subspace is a subspace</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>T:V\to W</m> is a linear map and <m>W_0\subset W</m> is a subspace, then its preimage, <m>T^{-1}(W_0)</m> is a subspace of <m>V</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
        <proof>
            <p>
                Try a direct proof. Allow the definitions to structure your proof. You may find <xref ref="def-preimage"/>, <xref ref="def-subspace"/>, and <xref ref="def-linearmap"/> useful.
            </p>
        </proof>
        <p>
            Please note that <m>T</m> need not be invertible and the symbol <m>T^{-1}</m> used in this context does not represent inversion. The preimage is something different - it is a set satisfying certain properties.
        </p>
        <definition xml:id="def-kernel">
            <title>Kernel</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>T:V\to W</m> is a linear map, then the <term>kernel</term> (a.k.a. <term>null space</term>) of <m>T</m> is the preimage of the trivial subspace. In symbols,
                </p>
                <me>\mathrm{ker}(T):= \mathrm{Null}(T) := T^{-1}(\vect{0}_W) = \{\vect{v}\in V \ | \ T(\vect{v}) = \vect{0}_W\}.</me>
            </statement>
        </definition>
        <corollary xml:id="cor-kernel_is_subspace">
            <title>Kernel is a subspace</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>T:V\to W</m> is a linear map, then <m>\mathrm{ker}(T)</m> is a vector subspace of <m>V</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </corollary>
        <proof>
            <p>
                You may find <xref ref="prop-trivial_subspace"/> and <xref ref="prop-preimage_of_subspace_is_subspace"/> useful.
            </p>
        </proof>
        <p>
            It turns out that the kernel of a linear map plays an important and recurring role throughout linear algebra.
        </p>

        <insight>
        <p>
            In this investigation, it was not technically necessary to draw any sort of cartoons or visualizations. However, when reasoning about images and preimages, it is often very useful and to have certain cartoons or metaphorical visualizations in mind.
            Carefully reflect upon this and then produce (and include) a metaphorical visualization that is clearly labelled and clearly depicts the following things: vector spaces <m>V</m> and <m>W</m>, the linear map <m>T:V\to W</m>, subsets <m>V_0\subset V</m> and <m>W_0\subset W</m>, <m>T(V_0)</m>, <m>T^{-1}(W_0)</m>, and <m>\ker(T)</m>.
            In what ways does this visualization support your reasoning about the above propositions?
        </p>
        </insight>

</section>








